					<item>
				<guid>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-359137#post-1787461</guid>
				<title>(no title)</title>
				<link>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-359137/scp-620#post-1787461</link>
				<description></description>
				<pubDate>Sat, 01 Jun 2013 05:19:05 +0000</pubDate>
				<wikidot:authorName>DoomZero</wikidot:authorName>				<wikidot:authorUserId>1384704</wikidot:authorUserId>				<content:encoded>
					<![CDATA[
						 <p>I love the fact that it appears at first to be a temporal SCP but really has no effect on time. It's entirely in the eye of the beholder.</p> <p>Another thing I like about it is the fact that it's set up as something which in its &quot;initial&quot; report, it was perceived as something totally beneficial with no harmful side-effects, and then the Foundation realizes there are drawbacks and potential dangers to it. As I was reading it, I thought &quot;Hey, you know, this sounds like it could be a really useful thing. It should help with productivity.&quot; Then, I realized that it would not be very beneficial if it lost power.</p> <p>Also, its neat little effect of randomly drawing more or less power is strange. Perhaps my understanding of physics is subpar here, but wouldn't something undergoing a sudden personal time acceleration draw more power into it? Hypothetically, I mean, if this SCP didn't affect just the mind, and its actual inner workings were affected by random time accleration/dilation, that would explain it using more or less power. It contradicts the stated fact that the clock's effects are purely &quot;psychosomatic&quot;.</p> <p>Regardless of the above thought, I up-voted this article.</p> 
				 	]]>
				</content:encoded>							</item>
